{"qid": "gf-13", "paper_id": "2001.06899", "answer": true, "unanimous": false, "quote": "Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the \\mathup{{{t}}} \\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}} rejection factor of ≈4.8 .", "all_quotes": ["Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the \\mathup{{{t}}} \\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}} rejection factor of ≈4.8 .", "Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the 𝑡𝑡¯ rejection factor of ≈4.8.", "Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the pTmiss selection (pTmiss <40 GeV) efficiencies for Z+jets and Z+HF jets events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in Z+HF jets events, which results in large pTmiss values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for Z+HF jets events by ≈1% at high jet and Z boson pT regions compared to those of Z+jets events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet (Z boson) pT bins where pT >60 (90) GeV .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)\\, GeV ."], "answers": ["The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen because semileptonic decays of heavy-flavor (HF) hadrons in Z+HF jets events produce large MET values, leading to lower selection efficiencies for those events compared to Z+jets events. This difference in efficiency (≈1% at high-pT regions) necessitates the cut to account for systematic uncertainties, with an uncertainty of 1.5% applied for high-pT bins.", "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to achieve ~80% signal efficiency while providing a ~4.8x rejection of $t\\overline{t}$ background.", "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to achieve ~80% signal efficiency while providing a ~4.8x rejection of tt̄ background.", "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to reduce background from top quark processes (t t̄ and single top) due to their neutrino-induced missing energy. This cut maintains ~80% signal efficiency while achieving a ~4.8x rejection of top quark backgrounds."], "votes": {"False": 26, "True": 4}, "escalated": false, "sections_read": [" + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "Appendix B Measured differential cross section ratios", "Cross section ratio measurements + Analysis strategy + $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ +HF jets event tagging efficiency", "Estimation of the event yields", "Object reconstruction and event selection", "Results + Summary + Appendix A Post-fit $M_{\\text{SV}}$ distributions in the exclusive jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins", "Systematic uncertainties", "Unfolding"], "verdicts": [{"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-13", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "quotes": [], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The paper does not discuss the choice of the MET cut of 40 GeV in the provided sections.", "section": " + Abstract + Introduction + The CMS detector + Data and simulated samples", "window": 0, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text does not mention the MET cut of 40 GeV or any reasoning related to its choice. The provided sections discuss jet and lepton transverse momentum cuts (pT>30 GeV and pT>25 GeV), but no MET requirements are specified.\", \"quotes\": [], \"answer\": \"The paper does not discuss the choice of the MET cut of 40 GeV in the provided sections.\", \"found\": false}"}, {"paper_