 , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the \\mathup{{{t}}} \\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}} rejection factor of ≈4.8 ."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to reduce background from top quark processes (t t̄ and single top) due to their neutrino-induced missing energy. This cut maintains ~80% signal efficiency while achieving a ~4.8x rejection of top quark backgrounds.", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 1, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text explains the MET cut choice in the context of reducing background from top quark processes and its effect on signal efficiency and background rejection.\", \"quotes\": [\"Its magnitude, p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}^{\\\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the \\\\mathup{{{t}}} \\\\mathup{{\\\\overline{{{\\\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}} rejection factor of ≈4.8 .\"], \"answer\": \"The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to reduce background from top quark processes (t t̄ and single top) due to their neutrino-induced missing energy. This cut maintains ~80% signal efficiency while achieving a ~4.8x rejection of top quark backgrounds.\", \"found\": true}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-13", "answer": true, "quote": "Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the 𝑡𝑡¯ rejection factor of ≈4.8.", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the 𝑡𝑡¯ rejection factor of ≈4.8."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to achieve ~80% signal efficiency while providing a ~4.8x rejection of tt̄ background.", "section": "Object reconstruction and event selection", "window": 1, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\"