": false}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-13", "answer": true, "quote": "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the pTmiss selection (pTmiss <40 GeV) efficiencies for Z+jets and Z+HF jets events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in Z+HF jets events, which results in large pTmiss values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for Z+HF jets events by ≈1% at high jet and Z boson pT regions compared to those of Z+jets events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet (Z boson) pT bins where pT >60 (90) GeV .", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the pTmiss selection (pTmiss <40 GeV) efficiencies for Z+jets and Z+HF jets events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in Z+HF jets events, which results in large pTmiss values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for Z+HF jets events by ≈1% at high jet and Z boson pT regions compared to those of Z+jets events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet (Z boson) pT bins where pT >60 (90) GeV ."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen because semileptonic decays of heavy-flavor (HF) hadrons in Z+HF jets events produce large MET values, leading to lower selection efficiencies for those events compared to Z+jets events. This difference in efficiency (≈1% at high-pT regions) necessitates the cut to account for systematic uncertainties, with an uncertainty of 1.5% applied for high-pT bins.", "section": "Systematic uncertainties", "window": 7, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions the MET cut (pTmiss <40 GeV) in the context of the 'Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency' systematic uncertainty. It explains that semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in Z+HF jets events produce large MET values, leading to lower efficiencies for those events compared to Z+jets events. The 40 GeV cut's role and choice are tied to accounting for these efficiency differences and associated uncertainties.\",\n\"quotes\": [\n\"Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the pTmiss selection (pTmiss <40 GeV) efficiencies for Z+jets and Z+HF jets events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in Z+HF jets events, which results in large pTmiss values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for Z+HF jets events by ≈1% at high jet and Z boson pT regions compared to those of Z+jets events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet (Z boson) pT bins where pT >60 (90) GeV .\"\n],\n\"answer\": \"The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen because semileptonic decays of heavy-flavor (HF) hadrons in Z+HF jets events produce large MET values, leading to lower selection efficiencies for those events compared to Z+jets events. This difference in efficiency (≈1% at high-pT regions) necessitates the cut to account for systematic uncertainties, with an uncertainty of 1.5% applied for high-pT bins.\",\n\"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-13", "answer": false, "quote": "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)\\, GeV .", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)\\, GeV ."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The text describes the 40 GeV MET cut's impact on efficiency differences between HF and non-HF jets due to semileptonic decays but does not explain why this specific value was chosen.", "section": "Systematic uncertainties", "window": 7, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions the MET cut of 40 GeV in the context of systematic uncer