y slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\\text{~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\\text{~jets}$ events. The ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{light~jets}$ mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The paper uses an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm for tagging. The b-jet tagging efficiency ranges from 45.9% to 60.7%, while the light-jet mistag rate ranges from 0.3% to 1.0% in the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range of 30–200 GeV. The working point is not explicitly stated.", "section": "Cross section ratio measurements + Analysis strategy + $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ +HF jets event tagging efficiency", "window": 3, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm used for SV tagging, which is part of the jet flavor identification process. It provides efficiencies for tagging b-jets (45.9-60.7%) and mistag rates for light jets (0.3-1.0%). The working point is not explicitly named.\", \"quotes\": [\"SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of $\\\\Delta R<0.3$ around the jet axis.\", \"In the jet $p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\\\hskip-2.5pt+\\\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\\\mathup{{{c}}}}\\\\text{~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\\\hskip-2.5pt+\\\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\\\mathup{{{b}}}}\\\\text{~jets}$ events. The ${\\\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\\\hskip-2.5pt+\\\\hskip-1.49994pt\\\\text{light~jets}$ mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same $p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}$ range.\"], \"answer\": \"The paper uses an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm for tagging. The b-jet tagging efficiency ranges from 45.9% to 60.7%, while the light-jet mistag rate ranges from 0.3% to 1.0% in the jet $p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}$ range of 30–200 GeV. The working point is not explicitly stated.\", \"found\": true}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": true, "quote": "The SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of ΔR<0.3 around the jet axis.", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of ΔR<0.3 around the jet axis.", "In the jet p_{mathrm{T}} range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for Z+c jets and from 45.9 to 60.7% for Z+b jets events. The Z+light jets mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same p_{mathrm{T}} range."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The paper uses an 'adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm' for SV tagging. The tagging efficiencies are 8.3–11.3% for c jets and 45.9–60.7% for b jets, with a light-jet mistag rate of 0.3–1.0%. No specific b-tagging working point is mentioned.", "section": "Cross section ratio measurements + Analysis strategy + $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ +HF jets event tagging efficiency", "window": 3, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions the 'adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm' used for SV reconstruction, which is part of the tagging process. It provides tagging efficiencies for b and c jets (45.9-60.7% and 8.3-11.3%, respectively) and the light-jet mistag rate (0.3-1.0%). However, it does not explicitly name a b-tagging algorithm (e.g., CSV, DeepJet) or specify a working point (e.g., loose, medium, tight).\", \"quotes\": [\"The SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of ΔR<0.3 around the jet axis.\", \"In the jet p_{mathrm{T}} range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for Z+c jets and from 45.9 to 60.7% for Z+b jets events. The Z+light jets mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same p_{mathrm{T}} range.\"], \"answer\": \"The paper uses an 'adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm' for SV tagging. The tagging efficiencies are 8.3–11.3% for c jets and 45.9–60.7% for b jets, with a light-jet mistag rate of 0.3–1.0%. No specific b-tagging working point is mentioned.\", \"found\": true}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": true, "quote": "The SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of $\\Delta R<0.3$ around the jet axis.", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of $\\Delta R<0.3$ around the jet axis.", "In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}~jets}$ events. The ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{light~jets}$ mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The paper uses an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm (referenced as [43]) for SV tagging. The tagging efficiencies for c jets range from 8.3% to 11.3%, and for b jets from 45.9% to 60.7% in the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range of 30-200 GeV. The mistagging rate for light jets increases from 0.3% to 1.0% in the same range. No specific b-tagging working point is mentioned.", "section": "Cross section ratio measurements + Analysis strategy + $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ +HF jets event tagging efficiency", "window": 3, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions the use of an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm for SV tagging and provides efficiencies for tagging c and b jets, along with mistag rates for light jets. However, it does not explicitly name a specific b-tagging algorithm (e.g., CSV, DeepJet) or a working point (e.g., loose, medium, tight).\", \"quotes\": [\"The SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of $\\\\Delta R<0.3$ around the jet axis.\", \"In the jet $p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\\\hskip-2.5pt+\\\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\\\mathup{{{c}}}~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\\\hskip-2.5pt+\\\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\\\mathup{{{b}}}~jets}$ events. The ${\\\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\\\hskip-2.5pt+\\\\hskip-1.49994pt{light~jets}$ mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same $p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}$ range.\"], \"answer\": \"The paper uses an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm (referenced as [43]) for SV tagging. The tagging efficiencies for c jets range from 8.3% to 11.3%, and for b jets from 45.9% to 60.7% in the jet $p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}$ range of 30-200 GeV. The mistagging rate for light jets increases from 0.3% to 1.0% in the same range. No specific b-tagging working point is mentioned.\", \"found\": true}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": true, "quote": "The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure. To reduce the combinatorics the best pair (triplet) of jets is chosen by minimizing the reconstructed and nominal mass of the $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson (top quark). From this optimization, the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet candidates from top quark and $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson decays are identified. The event is kept if these candidates pass the jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4 .", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure. To reduce the combinatorics the best pair (triplet) of jets is chosen by minimizing the reconstructed and nominal mass of the $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson (top quark). From this optimization, the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet candidates from top quark and $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson decays are identified. The event is kept if these candidates pass the jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4 .", "In the resulting sample of the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jet candidates about half have correct flavor assignment whereas the other half constitute mostly $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets that are misidentified as $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets.", "The $M_{\text{SV}}$ template for $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets is derived from a high-purity data sample of $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ events decaying to final states of ${\\mathup{{{e}}}}{\\mathup{}}$ $+\\geq$ 2 jets with at least one b-tagged jet."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The text refers to an unspecified 'HF tagging requirement' for b/c jet identification. Validation shows ~50% of c-jet candidates are correctly identified, with the rest mostly misidentified b-jets. b-jets are selected using a top-antitop sample requiring at least one b-tagged jet. No explicit algorithm name or working point is provided.", "section": "Estimation of the event yields", "window": 4, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions the 'HF tagging requirement' and validation samples for c and b jets, but does not explicitly name the b-tagging algorithm or its working point. It provides performance information about the fraction of correctly identified jets and misidentification rates in validation samples.\",\n\"quotes\": [\n\"The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure. To reduce the combinatorics the best pair (triplet) of jets is chosen by minimizing the reconstructed and nominal mass of the $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson (top quark). From this optimization, the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet candidates from top quark and $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson decays are identified. The event is kept if these candidates pass the jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4 .\",\n\"In the resulting sample of the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jet candidates about half have correct flavor assignment whereas the other half constitute mostly $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets that are misidentified as $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets.\",\n\"The $M_{\\text{SV}}$ template for $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets is derived from a high-purity data sample of $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ events decaying to final states of ${\\mathup{{{e}}}}{\\mathup{}}$ $+\\geq$ 2 jets with at least one b-tagged jet.\"\n],\n\"answer\": \"The text refers to an unspecified 'HF tagging requirement' for b/c jet identification. Validation shows ~50% of c-jet candidates are correctly identified, with the rest mostly misidentified b-jets. b-jets are selected using a top-antitop sample requiring at least one b-tagged jet. No explicit algorithm name or working point is provided.\",\n\"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": false, "quote": "The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure.", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure.", "From this optimization, the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet candidates from top quark and $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson decays are identified. The event is kept if these candidates pass the jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4 .", "In the resulting sample of the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jet candidates about half have correct flavor assignment whereas the other half constitute mostly $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets that are misidentified as $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets.", "Good agreement between the data-driven template shape and the simulation is observed."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The paper refers to 'HF-tagged jets' using a procedure described in Section 4 (not provided here). The c and b jet identification involves a selection based on minimizing mass differences, with events requiring passing an unspecified HF tagging requirement. Performance includes a 50% misidentification rate of b jets as c jets in validation samples and agreement between data and simulation for $M_{\\text{SV}}$ distributions.", "section": "Estimation of the event yields", "window": 4, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions 'HF-tagged jets' and refers to a 'jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4,' but Section 4 is not included here. The c and b jet identification procedure is described, but the specific algorithm or working point is not named. Performance is indirectly mentioned in terms of misidentification rates (e.g., half of c jet candidates are misidentified b jets) and agreement between data and simulation for M_SV distributions, but no explicit efficiency or working point performance metrics are provided.\",\n\"quotes\": [\n\"The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure.\",\n\"From this optimization, the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet candidates from top quark and $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson decays are identified. The event is kept if these candidates pass the jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4 .\",\n\"In the resulting sample of the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jet candidates about half have correct flavor assignment whereas the other half constitute mostly $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets that are misidentified as $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets.\",\n\"Good agreement between the data-driven template shape and the simulation is observed.\"\n],\n\"answer\": \"The paper refers to 'HF-tagged jets' using a procedure described in Section 4 (not provided here). The c and b jet identification involves a selection based on minimizing mass differences, with events requiring passing an unspecified HF tagging requirement. Performance includes a 50% misidentification rate of b jets as c jets in validation samples and agreement between data and simulation for $M_{\\\\text{SV}}$ distributions.\",\n\"found\": false\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": false, "quote": "The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure...", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure...", "The event is kept if these candidates pass the jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4 .", "In the resulting sample of the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jet candidates about half have correct flavor assignment whereas the other half constitute mostly $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets that are misidentified as $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The paper refers to 'HF-tagged jets' and a 'jet HF tagging requirement' without specifying the algorithm or working point. It notes that half of c-jet candidates are misidentified b-jets, but provides no explicit performance metrics (e.g., efficiency, mistag rate). Scale factors (SF_c=0.849±0.013±0.064, SF_b=0.873±0.005±0.013) adjust MC to data, but these are normalization factors, not b-tagging performance.", "section": "Estimation of the event yields", "window": 4, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions 'HF-tagged jets' and a 'jet HF tagging requirement' but does not specify the b-tagging algorithm or working point. It discusses validation of c and b jet identification using data samples and mentions misidentification rates (e.g., half of c jet candidates are misidentified b jets), but no explicit performance metrics (like efficiency or working point names) are provided. The scale factors (SF_c and SF_b) adjust MC to data, but these are normalization factors, not b-tagging performance.\", \"quotes\": [\"The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure...\", \"The event is kept if these candidates pass the jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4 .\", \"In the resulting sample of the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jet candidates about half have correct flavor assignment whereas the other half constitute mostly $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets that are misidentified as $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets.\"], \"answer\": \"The paper refers to 'HF-tagged jets' and a 'jet HF tagging requirement' without specifying the algorithm or working point. It notes that half of c-jet candidates are misidentified b-jets, but provides no explicit performance metrics (e.g., efficiency, mistag rate). Scale factors (SF_c=0.849±0.013±0.064, SF_b=0.873±0.005±0.013) adjust MC to data, but these are normalization factors, not b-tagging performance.\", \"found\": false}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-11", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "quotes": [], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "No information about the b-tagging algorithm, working point, or its performance is explicitly stated in the provided text.", "section": "Estimation of the event yields", "window": 5, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The provided text includes tables discussing scale factors (SF_c and SF_b) for c and b jets in Z pT bins, along with fractions of Z+c and Z+b jets. However, it does not explicitly mention any b-tagging algorithm name, working point, or performance metrics (e.g., efficiency, mistag rate). The figure caption references secondary vertex invariant mass distributions, which are associated with b-tagging techniques, but this is not explicitly tied to an algorithm or working point in the text provided.\", \"quotes\": [], \"answer\": \"No information about the b-tagging algorithm, working point, or its performance is explicitly stated in the provided text.\", \"fou