{"qid": "gf-14", "paper_id": "2006.05880", "answer": true, "unanimous": false, "quote": "Searches for top squark production in events involving Higgs or $Z$ bosons have been performed previously by both ATLAS [ 20 , 21 ] and CMS [ 22 , 23 ] .", "all_quotes": ["Searches for top squark production in events involving Higgs or $Z$ bosons have been performed previously by both ATLAS [ 20 , 21 ] and CMS [ 22 , 23 ] .", "For each signal hypothesis, the background fit is repeated taking into account the signal contamination in the CRs, which is found to be below 12% for signal models close to the existing exclusion limits [ 20 ] .", "For signal models close to the existing exclusion limits [ 20 ] .", "These results improve upon the existing limits on the $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass in this model by approximately 300 GeV [ 20 ].", "Results are compared with the observed limits obtained by the previous ATLAS search in Ref. [ 20 ] .", "extending beyond the previous limits on the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass from Ref. [ 89 ] by up to 160 GeV.", "Compared with previous limits, these results extend the mass parameter space exclusion by up to 300 GeV in $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass and by up to 160 GeV in $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass in the considered $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ model.", "The limits exclude, at 95% confidence level, $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ masses up to 1220 GeV in models featuring $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ production and $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to Z/h\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ decays, and $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ masses up to 875 GeV in models featuring $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ production and $\\tilde{t}_{2}\\to Z\\tilde{t}_{1}$ with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise .64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ decays."], "answers": ["The 2006.05880 analysis extends previous ATLAS limits by up to 300 GeV in $\\tilde{t}_1$ mass and up to 160 GeV in $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$ mass in the $\\tilde{t}_2$ model.", "The 2006.05880 analysis's stop-mass limits improve upon the previous ATLAS result by approximately 300 GeV, as stated in the text. The comparison is explicitly made with Ref. [20], which is cited as the prior ATLAS search.", "The new analysis extends the previous ATLAS limits (Ref. [89]) on the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass by up to 160 GeV.", "The new analysis extends the previous ATLAS limits (Ref. [89]) on the $\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass by up to 160 GeV.", "The new results extend previous ATLAS limits by excluding up to 300 GeV more in $\\tilde{t}_1$ mass and 160 GeV more in $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$ mass in specific models.", "The new results extend the previous limits on the $\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass from Ref. [89] by up to 160 GeV.", "The paper acknowledges prior ATLAS searches (references [20,21]) but do