answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "quotes": [], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The paper does not mention the signal efficiency of the pT^miss < 40 GeV cut.", "section": "Results + Summary + Appendix A Post-fit $M_{\\text{SV}}$ distributions in the exclusive jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins", "window": 8, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text does not mention the pT^miss < 40 GeV cut or any signal efficiency related to it. The discussion focuses on jet pT, Z boson pT, and other kinematic variables, but missing transverse momentum is not addressed in the provided sections.\", \"quotes\": [], \"answer\": \"The paper does not mention the signal efficiency of the pT^miss < 40 GeV cut.\", \"found\": false}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "quotes": [], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The text does not mention signal efficiency for a pT^miss < 40 GeV cut.", "section": "Appendix B Measured differential cross section ratios", "window": 9, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text provides tables of cross section ratios in jet pT and Z boson pT bins, but there is no mention of pT^miss (missing transverse energy) cuts or signal efficiency values related to a pT^miss < 40 GeV cut. The question's specific criteria are not addressed in the provided content.\", \"quotes\": [], \"answer\": \"The text does not mention signal efficiency for a pT^miss < 40 GeV cut.\", \"found\": false}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "quotes": [], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The text does not mention signal efficiency for a pT^miss < 40 GeV cut.", "section": "Appendix B Measured differential cross section ratios", "window": 9, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The question asks about the signal efficiency retained by the pT^miss < 40 GeV cut. The provided text includes tables (Table 18 and Table 20) discussing cross section ratios in jet pT and Z boson pT bins, but there is no mention of pT^miss or any cuts related to it. The terms 'signal efficiency' or 'pT^miss' do not appear in the text.\", \"quotes\": [], \"answer\": \"The text does not mention signal efficiency for a pT^miss < 40 GeV cut.\", \"found\": false}"}, {"paper_id": "2001.06899", "qid": "gf-10", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "quotes": [], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The text does not mention signal efficiency for the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} < 40$ GeV cut.", "section": "Appendix B Measured differential cross section ratios", "window": 9, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text provided includes tables with cross section ratios in jet and Z boson $p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}$ bins, but none of the content mentions $p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}^{\\\\text{miss}}$ cuts or signal efficiency related to them. The question's focus on $p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}^{\\\\text{miss}} < 40$ GeV is not addressed in the given sections.\", \"quotes\": [], \"answer\": \"The text does not mention signal efficiency for the $p_{\\\\mathrm{T}}^{\\\\text{miss}} < 40$ GeV cut.\", \"found\": false}"}]}
{"qid": "gf-06", "paper_id": "2006.05880", "answer": true, "unanimous": true, "quote": "Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production ( $t\\bar{t}$ ) background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ ( $\\ell\\equiv e,\\mu$ ) decay or a pair of $b$ -tagged jets originating from the $h\\to b\\bar{b}$ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "all_quotes": ["Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production ( $t\\bar{t}$ ) background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ ( $\\ell\\equiv e,\\mu$ ) decay or a pair of $b$ -tagged jets originating from the $h\\to b\\bar{b}$ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production (t t̄) background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the Z→ℓ+ℓ− (ℓ≡e,μ) decay or a pair of b-tagged jets originating from the h→b b̄ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 $\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving $Z$ bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 $\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).", "The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).", "The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol ℓ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the Z boson mass (|m_{ℓℓ}−m_Z|<15 GeV, with m_Z=91.2 GeV) is required.", "Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction.", "Signal region SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ is optimised for large $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}-\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_1\\to t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to h/Z\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model.", "Models with small mass differences between the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ are targeted with SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair ( $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ ).", "Two SRs are optimised for the $\\tilde{t}_2\\to Z\\tilde{t}_1$ with $\\tilde{t}_1\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ , targeting small and large mass splittings between the $\\tilde{t}_2$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ , respectively.", "SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ features upper bounds on the $p_{\\text{T}}$ of the third leading lepton and on $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ , as well as no requirement on the number of $b$ -tagged jets since they are likely to be soft in this scenario.", "SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ also includes an upper bound on the third leading lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ but requires the presence of $b$ -tagged jets and large $E_{\\text{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ and $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ .", "Models with small mass differences between the [...] are targeted with SR^Z_{1B}, which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair (p_{T}^{ℓℓ}).", "Two SRs are optimised for the [...] model, SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B}, targeting small and large mass splittings between the [...] respectively.", "Due to the overall soft kinematics [...] SR^Z_{2A} [...] features upper bounds [...] SR^Z_{2B} [...] requires the presence of b-tagged jets and large E_T^{miss} and p_{T}^{ℓℓ}.", "The requirement on the number of signal leptons [...] makes the SR^Z_{1A} and SR^Z_{1B} selections insensitive to potential contributions [...] The acceptance [...] was found to depend linearly [...] The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution [...] to SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B}.", "The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol $\\ell$ ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the $Z$ boson mass ( $|m_{\\ell\\ell}-m_{Z}|<15$ GeV , with $m_{Z}=91.2$ GeV ) is required.", "Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction .", "Signal region SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ is optimised for large $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}-\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to h/Z\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model.", "Two SRs are optimised for the $\\tilde{t}_{2}\\to Z\\tilde{t}_{1}$ with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ , targeting small and large mass splittings between the $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ , respectively.", "The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes the SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays...", "The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The dominant SM background contribution to the $3\\ell$ SRs is expected to originate from $t\\bar{t}Z$ production, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\bar{t}$ events).", "The normalisation of the main backgrounds ( $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson in the $3\\ell$ selection; $t\\bar{t}$ in the $1\\ell$ selection) is obtained by fitting the yield from MC simulation to the observed data in dedicated control regions (CRs) enhanced in a particular background component, and then extrapolating this yield to the SRs.", "Analogously to the 3 $\\ell$ selection, the acceptance for mixed decay scenarios with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\tilde{t}_{1}\\rightarrow t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ has been found to depend linearly on the branching fraction of $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ to $t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ .", "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The estimates for the FNP background are validated in dedicated regions with selection criteria similar to those defining the 3 $\\ell$ SRs but with reduced contributions from processes with three prompt leptons.", "Requirement / Region\nVR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$\n\nVR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$", "The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6 .", "Requirement / Region\nCR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$\n\nVR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$\n\nCR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$\n\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$\n\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$", "To validate the background estimates and provide a statistically independent cross-check of the extrapolation to the SRs, three validation regions are defined, as shown in Table 6 . The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.", "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4, with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "To enhance fakes and be mutually exclusive with the SR, events in these VRs are required to have no SF-OS dilepton pairs and to have at least one different-flavour opposite-sign (DF-OS) dilepton pair.", "Number of signal leptons ≥ 3 (from Table 6 for CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, CR^Z_{VV}, VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}, and VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}})", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 $\\ell$ and 1 $\\ell$ SRs, respectively.", "To enhance the exclusion power in the SUSY models considered, a ‘shape-fit’ approach is used where several mutually exclusive bins in different kinematic variables are defined in order to take advantage of the different signal-to-background ratios in the different bins.", "The likelihood is built from the product of probability density functions describing the observed numbers of events in the SR and the associated CRs.", "Table 10 also shows upper limits (at the 95% CL) on the number of BSM events $S^{95}$ , and on the visible BSM cross-section $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{vis}}$", "The statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 .", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 ℓ and 1 ℓ SRs, respectively.", "Table 10: Observed and expected numbers of events in the 3 ℓ signal regions...", "Table 12: Selection criteria used in the shape-fit to derive the model-dependent exclusion limits...", "The shape-fit approach is used where several mutually exclusive bins in different kinematic variables are defined in order to take advantage of the different signal-to-background ratios in the different bins.", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 .", "A search for direct top squark pair production in events with a leptonically decaying $Z$ boson or a SM Higgs boson decaying into a $b$ -quark pair is presented."], "answers": ["All four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in a specific model using m^{3ℓ}_{T2}; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences using p_T^{ℓℓ}; SR^Z_{2A} targets small mass splittings in another model with soft kinematics (upper pT bounds); SR^Z_{2B} targets large mass splittings in that model with b-tagged jets and higher missing ET/p_T^{ℓℓ}.", "All four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_1$ model; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences between $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_2$ and $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$; SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B} target small and large mass splittings between $\\tilde{t}_2$ and $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$, respectively. Each uses specific kinematic requirements (e.g., $m^{3\\ell}_{\\text{T}2}$, $p_{\\text{T}}$ bounds, $b$-tags) to optimize sensitivity for their respective models.", "Regions requiring ≥3 leptons and their roles: \n- VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F}: Validate FNP lepton background estimates by probing lepton pT regimes and soft lepton requirements, while excluding SF-OS dilepton pairs. \n- CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}: Estimates t\\bar{t}Z background with specific MET and jet requirements. \n- CR^Z_{VV}: Estimates multi-boson background with MET and b-tagging constraints. \n- VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}: Validates t\\bar{t}Z background estimates. \n- VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}} and VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}: Validate multi-boson background estimates, focusing on jet multiplicity and b-tagging.", "The 3ℓ (three-lepton) region requires at least three leptons, and its role is to target top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The 3ℓ SRs (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles include setting exclusion limits for BSM scenarios via upper limits on BSM events and cross-sections (via Tables 10 and 12), and enhancing SUSY model exclusion power through shape-fit analyses of kinematic distributions (as in Figures 6 and 9).", "The 3ℓ SRs (three-lepton signal regions) require at least three leptons. Their role is related to background estimation, specifically targeting backgrounds like $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson processes.", "The 3ℓ SRs (three-lepton signal regions) require at least three leptons. Their role is to estimate Standard Model backgrounds (e.g., $t\\bar{t}Z$, multi-boson, fake/non-prompt leptons) by using control regions and Monte Carlo simulations for normalization and validation.", "The 3ℓ SRs require at least three leptons. Their role is implied to be signal regions where backgrounds (e.g., ttZ, multi-boson, fake/non-prompt leptons) are estimated, but the text does not explicitly describe their specific roles beyond their existence in the analysis.", "The 3ℓ region requires at least three leptons and is aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The 3ℓ region requires at least three leptons, and its role is to target top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The region requiring at least three leptons uses an SF-OS lepton pair from $Z$ decay and an additional lepton from top quark decay.", "The region requiring three leptons is when an SF-OS lepton pair (from Z decay) and an additional lepton (from top quark decay) are present. The SF-OS pair identifies the Z boson decay, while the additional lepton helps distinguish the signal from background.", "The regions requiring at least three leptons are SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, and SR^Z_{2B}. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in the first model using m^{3ℓ}_{T2}; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences using p_T^{ℓℓ}; SR^Z_{2A} targets small mass splittings in the second model with upper pT bounds and no b-tag requirements; SR^Z_{2B} targets large mass splittings in the second model with b-tagged jets and high E_T^{miss}/p_T^{ℓℓ}.", "The regions requiring at least three leptons are the 3ℓ SRs (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}). Their roles involve contributing to exclusion limits for BSM/SUSY models via kinematic bins (e.g., E_T^miss, p_T^ℓℓ) to differentiate signal/background ratios, as detailed in Tables 10/12 and figures 6/8.", "The regions requiring at least three leptons are: (1) VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} (validating FNP lepton background estimates with criteria similar to signal regions but reduced prompt lepton contributions), (2) CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z} (estimating t\\bar{t}Z background), (3) CR^Z_{VV} (estimating multi-boson background), and (4) VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, and VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag} (validating t\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson background estimates via jet multiplicity/extrapolation checks).", "The regions requiring three leptons include those with an SF-OS lepton pair from Z decay and an additional lepton from top quark decay, or a b-tagged jet pair from Higgs decay plus an additional lepton. The SF-OS pair identifies the Z boson, the b-tagged jets identify the Higgs boson, and the additional lepton comes from top quark decay.", "The regions requiring ≥3 leptons are: (1) VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} (for validating fake/non-prompt lepton backgrounds), (2) CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z} and CR^Z_{VV} (for estimating t\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson backgrounds), and (3) VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, and VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag} (for validating those estimates)."], "votes": {"True": 17, "False": 52}, "escalated": false, "sections_read": [" + Introduction", "1 $\\ell$ selection + Background estimation", "3 $\\ell$ selection", "ATLAS detector + Data set and simulated event samples", "Conclusion", "Estimation of the $t\\bar{t}$ background in the 1 $\\ell$ selection + Systematic uncertainties", "Event selection", "Fake/non-prompt lepton background + Estimation of the $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson background in the 3 $\\ell$ selection", "Results"], "verdicts": [{"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production ( $t\\bar{t}$ ) background by 