ast three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons)' and 'exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons)'. The question asks specifically about regions requiring *at least three leptons*, so only the 3ℓ category is relevant here. The role of this region is explicitly stated in the text.\", \"quotes\": [\"The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 $\\\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving $Z$ bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 $\\\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).\"], \"answer\": \"The 3ℓ region requires at least three leptons, and its role is to target top squark decays involving Z bosons.\", \"found\": true}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 $\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving $Z$ bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 $\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 $\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving $Z$ bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 $\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons)."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The 3ℓ (three-lepton) region requires at least three leptons, and its role is to target top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "section": "Event selection", "window": 2, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions two event categories: 'at least three leptons (3ℓ selection)' and 'exactly one lepton (1ℓ selection)'. The 3ℓ category explicitly requires at least three leptons, and its role is stated as targeting top squark decays involving Z bosons. The question asks only about regions requiring at least three leptons, so the 1ℓ category is irrelevant here.\",\n\"quotes\": [\"The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 $\\\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving $Z$ bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 $\\\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).\"],\n\"answer\": \"The 3ℓ (three-lepton) region requires at least three leptons, and its role is to target top squark decays involving Z bosons.\",\n\"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons)."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The 3ℓ region requires at least three leptons and is aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "section": "Event selection", "window": 2, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions regions classified into categories requiring at least three leptons (3ℓ) and their purpose. The question asks which regions require three leptons and their role.\", \"quotes\": [\"The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).\"], \"answer\": \"The 3ℓ region requires at least three leptons and is aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons.\", \"found\": true}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol ℓ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the Z boson mass (|m_{ℓℓ}−m_Z|<15 GeV, with m_Z=91.2 GeV) is required.", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol ℓ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the Z boson mass (|m_{ℓℓ}−m_Z|<15 GeV, with m_Z=91.2 GeV) is required.", "Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction.", "Signal region SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ is optimised for large $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}-\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_1\\to t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to h/Z\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model.", "Models with small mass differences between the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ are targeted with SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair ( $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ ).", "Two SRs are optimised for the $\\tilde{t}_2\\to Z\\tilde{t}_1$ with $\\tilde{t}_1\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ , targeting small and large mass splittings between the $\\tilde{t}_2$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ , respectively.", "SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ features upper bounds on the $p_{\\text{T}}$ of the third leading lepton and on $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ , as well as no requirement on the number of $b$ -tagged jets since they are likely to be soft in this scenario.", "SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ also includes an upper bound on the third leading lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ but requires the presence of $b$ -tagged jets and large $E_{\\text{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ and $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ ."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "All four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in a specific model using m^{3ℓ}_{T2}; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences using p_T^{ℓℓ}; SR^Z_{2A} targets small mass splittings in another model with soft kinematics (upper pT bounds); SR^Z_{2B} targets large mass splittings in that model with b-tagged jets and higher missing ET/p_T^{ℓℓ}.", "section": "3 $\\ell$ selection", "window": 3, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"The text describes four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) in the 3ℓ selection, all requiring at least three leptons. Each region's role is specified based on targeted mass splittings and model scenarios.\",\n\"quotes\": [\n\"The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol ℓ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the Z boson mass (|m_{ℓℓ}−m_Z|<15 GeV, with m_Z=91.2 GeV) is required.\",\n\"Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction.\",\n\"Signal region SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ is optimised for large $\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}-\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ mass splittings in the $\\\\tilde{t}_1\\\\to t\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\\\to h/Z\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model.\",\n\"Models with small mass differences between the $\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$ and the $\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ are targeted with SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ , which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair ( $p_{\\\\textrm{T}}^{\\\\ell\\\\ell}$ ).\",\n\"Two SRs are optimised for the $\\\\tilde{t}_2\\\\to Z\\\\tilde{t}_1$ with $\\\\tilde{t}_1\\\\to bff^{\\\\prime}\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ , targeting small and large mass splittings between the $\\\\tilde{t}_2$ and the $\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ , respectively.\",\n\"SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ features upper bounds on the $p_{\\\\text{T}}$ of the third leading lepton and on $p_{\\\\textrm{T}}^{\\\\ell\\\\ell}$ , as well as no requirement on the number of $b$ -tagged jets since they are likely to be soft in this scenario.\",\n\"SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ also includes an upper bound on the third leading lepton $p_{\\\\text{T}}$ but requires the presence of $b$ -tagged jets and large $E_{\\\\text{T}}^{\\\\text{miss}}$ and $p_{\\\\textrm{T}}^{\\\\ell\\\\ell}$ .\"\n],\n\"answer\": \"All four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in a specific model using m^{3ℓ}_{T2}; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences using p_T^{ℓℓ}; SR^Z_{2A} targets small mass splittings in another model with soft kinematics (upper pT bounds); SR^Z_{2B} targets large mass splittings in that model with b-tagged jets and higher missing ET/p_T^{ℓℓ}.\",\n\"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol ℓ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the Z boson mass (|m_{ℓℓ}−m_Z|<15 GeV, with m_Z=91.2 GeV) is required.", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol ℓ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the Z boson mass (|m_{ℓℓ}−m_Z|<15 GeV, with m_Z=91.2 GeV) is required.", "Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction.", "Models with small mass differences between the [...] are targeted with SR^Z_{1B}, which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair (p_{T}^{ℓℓ}).", "Two SRs are optimised for the [...] model, SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B}, targeting small and large mass splittings between the [...] respectively.", "Due to the overall soft kinematics [...] SR^Z_{2A} [...] features upper bounds [...] SR^Z_{2B} [...] requires the presence of b-tagged jets and large E_T^{miss} and p_{T}^{ℓℓ}.", "The requirement on the number of signal leptons [...] makes the SR^Z_{1A} and SR^Z_{1B} selections insensitive to potential contributions [...] The acceptance [...] was found to depend linearly [...] The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution [...] to SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B}."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The regions requiring at least three leptons are SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, and SR^Z_{2B}. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in the first model using m^{3ℓ}_{T2}; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences using p_T^{ℓℓ}; SR^Z_{2A} targets small mass splittings in the second model with upper pT bounds and no b-tag requirements; SR^Z_{2B} targets large mass splittings in the second model with b-tagged jets and high E_T^{miss}/p_T^{ℓℓ}.", "section": "3 $\\ell$ selection", "window": 3, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"The text specifies that the 3ℓ selection requires at least three signal leptons. The four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) are part of this selection. Each region's role is described in terms of targeting specific simplified models and mass splittings between particles. The table confirms the three-lepton requirement for all regions, and the prose explains their purposes based on model parameters.\",\n\"quotes\": [\n\"The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol ℓ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the Z boson mass (|m_{ℓℓ}−m_Z|<15 GeV, with m_Z=91.2 GeV) is required.\",\n\"Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction.\",\n\"Signal region SR^Z_{1A} is optimised for large [...] mass splittings in the [...] model.\",\n\"Models with small mass differences between the [...] are targeted with SR^Z_{1B}, which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair (p_{T}^{ℓℓ}).\",\n\"Two SRs are optimised for the [...] model, SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B}, targeting small and large mass splittings between the [...] respectively.\",\n\"Due to the overall soft kinematics [...] SR^Z_{2A} [...] features upper bounds [...] SR^Z_{2B} [...] requires the presence of b-tagged jets and large E_T^{miss} and p_{T}^{ℓℓ}.\",\n\"The requirement on the number of signal leptons [...] makes the SR^Z_{1A} and SR^Z_{1B} selections insensitive to potential contributions [...] The acceptance [...] was found to depend linearly [...] The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution [...] to SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B}.\"\n],\n\"answer\": \"The regions requiring at least three leptons are SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, and SR^Z_{2B}. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in the first model using m^{3ℓ}_{T2}; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences using p_T^{ℓℓ}; SR^Z_{2A} targets small mass splittings in the second model with upper pT bounds and no b-tag requirements; SR^Z_{2B} targets large mass splittings in the second model with b-tagged jets and high E_T^{miss}/p_T^{ℓℓ}.\",\n\"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": t