\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to h/Z\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model.", "Models with small mass differences between the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ are targeted with SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair ( $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ ).", "Two SRs are optimised for the $\\tilde{t}_{2}\\to Z\\tilde{t}_{1}$ with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ , targeting small and large mass splittings between the $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ , respectively.", "The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes the SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays...", "The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ ."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "All four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_1$ model; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences between $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_2$ and $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$; SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B} target small and large mass splittings between $\\tilde{t}_2$ and $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$, respectively. Each uses specific kinematic requirements (e.g., $m^{3\\ell}_{\\text{T}2}$, $p_{\\text{T}}$ bounds, $b$-tags) to optimize sensitivity for their respective models.", "section": "3 $\\ell$ selection", "window": 3, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"The text explicitly states that all four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Each region's role is described in the context of targeting specific simplified models and mass splittings between particles. The quotes confirm the lepton requirement and the roles of each region.\",\n  \"quotes\": [\n    \"The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol $\\\\ell$ ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the $Z$ boson mass ( $|m_{\\\\ell\\\\ell}-m_{Z}|<15$ GeV , with $m_{Z}=91.2$ GeV ) is required.\",\n    \"Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction .\",\n    \"Signal region SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ is optimised for large $\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}-\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ mass splittings in the $\\\\tilde{t}_{1}\\\\to t\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\\\to h/Z\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model.\",\n    \"Models with small mass differences between the $\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{2}$ and the $\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ are targeted with SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ , which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair ( $p_{\\\\textrm{T}}^{\\\\ell\\\\ell}$ ).\",\n    \"Two SRs are optimised for the $\\\\tilde{t}_{2}\\\\to Z\\\\tilde{t}_{1}$ with $\\\\tilde{t}_{1}\\\\to bff^{\\\\prime}\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptstyle\\\\raise 0.90417pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\raise 0.64583pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\scriptscriptstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ , targeting small and large mass splittings between the $\\\\tilde{t}_{2}$ and the $\\\\textstyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ , respectively.\",\n    \"The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes the SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\\\\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays...\",\n    \"The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\\\\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ .\"\n  ],\n  \"answer\": \"All four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in the $\\\\tilde{t}_1$ model; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences between $\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^0_2$ and $\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^0_1$; SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B} target small and large mass splittings between $\\\\tilde{t}_2$ and $\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^0_1$, respectively. Each uses specific kinematic requirements (e.g., $m^{3\\\\ell}_{\\\\text{T}2}$, $p_{\\\\text{T}}$ bounds, $b$-tags) to optimize sensitivity for their respective models.\",\n  \"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "The dominant SM background contribution to the $3\\ell$ SRs is expected to originate from $t\\bar{t}Z$ production, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\bar{t}$ events).", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The dominant SM background contribution to the $3\\ell$ SRs is expected to originate from $t\\bar{t}Z$ production, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\bar{t}$ events).", "The normalisation of the main backgrounds ( $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson in the $3\\ell$ selection; $t\\bar{t}$ in the $1\\ell$ selection) is obtained by fitting the yield from MC simulation to the observed data in dedicated control regions (CRs) enhanced in a particular background component, and then extrapolating this yield to the SRs."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The 3ℓ SRs (three-lepton signal regions) require at least three leptons. Their role is to estimate Standard Model backgrounds (e.g., $t\\bar{t}Z$, multi-boson, fake/non-prompt leptons) by using control regions and Monte Carlo simulations for normalization and validation.", "section": "1 $\\ell$ selection + Background estimation", "window": 4, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions '3ℓ SRs' (three-lepton signal regions) in the context of background estimation. It specifies their dominant backgrounds and the methods used to estimate them. The regions requiring three leptons are the '3ℓ SRs', and their role is related to background contributions and estimation in the analysis.\", \"quotes\": [\"The dominant SM background contribution to the $3\\\\ell$ SRs is expected to originate from $t\\\\bar{t}Z$ production, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\\\bar{t}$ events).\", \"The normalisation of the main backgrounds ( $t\\\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson in the $3\\\\ell$ selection; $t\\\\bar{t}$ in the $1\\\\ell$ selection) is obtained by fitting the yield from MC simulation to the observed data in dedicated control regions (CRs) enhanced in a particular background component, and then extrapolating this yield to the SRs.\"], \"answer\": \"The 3ℓ SRs (three-lepton signal regions) require at least three leptons. Their role is to estimate Standard Model backgrounds (e.g., $t\\\\bar{t}Z$, multi-boson, fake/non-prompt leptons) by using control regions and Monte Carlo simulations for normalization and validation.\", \"found\": true}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "The dominant SM background contribution to the $3\\ell$ SRs is expected to originate from $t\\bar{t}Z$ production, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\bar{t}$ events).", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The dominant SM background contribution to the $3\\ell$ SRs is expected to originate from $t\\bar{t}Z$ production, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\bar{t}$ events).", "Analogously to the 3 $\\ell$ selection, the acceptance for mixed decay scenarios with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\tilde{t}_{1}\\rightarrow t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ has been found to depend linearly on the branching fraction of $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ to $t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ ."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The 3ℓ SRs require at least three leptons. Their role is implied to be signal regions where backgrounds (e.g., ttZ, multi-boson, fake/non-prompt leptons) are estimated, but the text does not explicitly describe their specific roles beyond their existence in the analysis.", "section": "1 $\\ell$ selection + Background estimation", "window": 4, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions '3ℓ SRs' in the context of background estimation, indicating regions requiring three leptons. The '3ℓ selection' is referenced analogously to the 1ℓ selection but no further details on roles are provided in the given text.\", \"quotes\": [\"The dominant SM background contribution to the $3\\\\ell$ SRs is expected to originate from $t\\\\bar{t}Z$ production, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\\\bar{t}$ events).\", \"Analogously to the 3 $\\\\ell$ selection, the acceptance for mixed decay scenarios with $\\\\tilde{t}_{1}\\\\tilde{t}_{1}\\\\rightarrow t\\\\mathchoice{\\\\displaystyle\\\\raise 1.72218pt\\\\hbox{$\\\\displaystyle\\\\tilde{\\\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\\\textstyle\\\\raise 