, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\bar{t}$ events)."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The 3ℓ SRs (three-lepton signal regions) require at least three leptons. Their role is related to background estimation, specifically targeting backgrounds like $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson processes.", "section": "1 $\\ell$ selection + Background estimation", "window": 4, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions '3ℓ SRs' (three-lepton signal regions) in the background estimation section, indicating regions requiring at least three leptons. The role of these regions is not explicitly detailed in the provided text beyond their background contributions.\", \"quotes\": [\"The dominant SM background contribution to the $3\\\\ell$ SRs is expected to originate from $t\\\\bar{t}Z$ production, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\\\bar{t}$ events).\"], \"answer\": \"The 3ℓ SRs (three-lepton signal regions) require at least three leptons. Their role is related to background estimation, specifically targeting backgrounds like $t\\\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson processes.\", \"found\": true}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The estimates for the FNP background are validated in dedicated regions with selection criteria similar to those defining the 3 $\\ell$ SRs but with reduced contributions from processes with three prompt leptons.", "Requirement / Region\nVR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$\n\nVR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$", "The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6 .", "Requirement / Region\nCR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$\n\nVR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$\n\nCR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$\n\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$\n\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$", "To validate the background estimates and provide a statistically independent cross-check of the extrapolation to the SRs, three validation regions are defined, as shown in Table 6 . The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The regions requiring at least three leptons are: (1) VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} (validating FNP lepton background estimates with criteria similar to signal regions but reduced prompt lepton contributions), (2) CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z} (estimating t\\bar{t}Z background), (3) CR^Z_{VV} (estimating multi-boson background), and (4) VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, and VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag} (validating t\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson background estimates via jet multiplicity/extrapolation checks).", "section": "Fake/non-prompt lepton background + Estimation of the $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson background in the 3 $\\ell$ selection", "window": 5, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"The text discusses validation regions (VRs) and control regions (CRs) in the 3ℓ selection that require at least three leptons. These regions are defined in Tables 4 and 6, and their roles involve validating background estimates for fake/non-prompt leptons, t̄tZ, and multi-boson processes.\",\n  \"quotes\": [\n    \"Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ .\",\n    \"The estimates for the FNP background are validated in dedicated regions with selection criteria similar to those defining the 3 $\\\\ell$ SRs but with reduced contributions from processes with three prompt leptons.\",\n    \"Requirement / Region\\nVR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{F}}}$\\n\\nVR ${}^{Z}_{2\\\\textrm{F}}$\",\n    \"Number of signal leptons\\n$\\\\geq 3$\",\n    \"The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\\\\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6 .\",\n    \"Requirement / Region\\nCR ${}^{Z}_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}$\\n\\nVR ${}^{Z}_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}$\\n\\nCR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$\\n\\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,n-jet}}$\\n\\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,}b\\\\textrm{-tag}}$\",\n    \"Number of signal leptons\\n$\\\\geq 3$\",\n    \"To validate the background estimates and provide a statistically independent cross-check of the extrapolation to the SRs, three validation regions are defined, as shown in Table 6 . The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\\\\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,}b\\\\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.\"\n  ],\n  \"answer\": \"The regions requiring at least three leptons are: (1) VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} (validating FNP lepton background estimates with criteria similar to signal regions but reduced prompt lepton contributions), (2) CR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z} (estimating t\\\\bar{t}Z background), (3) CR^Z_{VV} (estimating multi-boson background), and (4) VR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, and VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag} (validating t\\\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson background estimates via jet multiplicity/extrapolation checks).\",\n  \"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4, with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4, with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "To enhance fakes and be mutually exclusive with the SR, events in these VRs are required to have no SF-OS dilepton pairs and to have at least one different-flavour opposite-sign (DF-OS) dilepton pair.", "Number of signal leptons ≥ 3 (from Table 6 for CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, CR^Z_{VV}, VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}, and VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}})", "The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6 .", "To validate the background estimates and provide a statistically independent cross-check of the extrapolation to the SRs, three validation regions are defined, as shown in Table 6 . The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.", "The estimates for the FNP background are validated in dedicated regions with selection criteria similar to those defining the 3 $\\ell$ SRs but with reduced contributions from processes with three prompt leptons."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "Regions requiring ≥3 leptons and their roles: \n- VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F}: Validate FNP lepton background estimates by probing lepton pT regimes and soft lepton requirements, while excluding SF-OS dilepton pairs. \n- CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}: Estimates t\\bar{t}Z background with specific MET and jet requirements. \n- CR^Z_{VV}: Estimates multi-boson background with MET and b-tagging constraints. \n- VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}: Validates t\\bar{t}Z background estimates. \n- VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}} and VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}: Validate multi-boson background estimates, focusing on jet multiplicity and b-tagging.", "section": "Fake/non-prompt lepton background + Estimation of the $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson background in the 3 $\\ell$ selection", "window": 5, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"The text discusses validation regions (VRs) and control regions (CRs) in the 3ℓ selection. The VRs VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} from Table 4, and CR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}, CR^Z_{VV}, and their VRs from Table 6 all require ≥3 signal leptons. Their roles include validating fake/non-prompt lepton backgrounds and estimating/validating t\\\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson backgrounds.\",\n\"quotes\": [\n\"Number of signal leptons ≥ 3 (from Table 4 for VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F})\",\n\"Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4, with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ .\",\n\"To enhance fakes and be mutually exclusive with the SR, events in these VRs are required to have no SF-OS dilepton pairs and to have at least one different-flavour opposite-sign (DF-OS) dilepton pair.\",\n\"Number of signal leptons ≥ 3 (from Table 6 for CR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}, CR^Z_{VV}, VR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,n-jet}}, and VR_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,}b\\\\textrm{-tag}})\",\n\"The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\\\\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6 .\",\n\"To validate the background estimates and provide a statistically independent cross-check of the extrapolation to the SRs, three validation regions are defined, as shown in Table 6 . The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\\\\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,}b\\\\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.\",\n\"The estimates for the FNP background are validated in dedicated regions with selection criteria similar to those defining the 3 $\\\\ell$ SRs but with reduced contributions from processes with three prompt leptons.\"\n],\n\"answer\": \"Regions requiring ≥3 leptons and their roles: \\n- VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F}: Validate FNP lepton background estimates by probing lepton pT regimes and soft lepton requirements, while excluding SF-OS dilepton pairs. \\n- CR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}: Estimates t\\\\bar{t}Z background with specific MET and jet requirements. \\n- CR^Z_{VV}: Estimates multi-boson background with MET and b-tagging constraints. \\n- VR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}: Validates t\\\\bar{t}Z background estimates. \\n- VR_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,n-jet}} and VR_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,}b\\\\textrm{-tag}}: Validate multi-boson background estimates, focusing on jet multiplicity and b-tagging.\",\n\"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "Requirement / Region\nVR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$\n\nVR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$", "To validate the background estimates and provide a statistically independent cross-check of the extrapolation to the SRs, three validation regions are defined, as shown in Table 6 . The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.", "Requirement / Region\nCR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$\n\nVR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$\n\nCR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$\n\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$\n\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$"], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The regions requiring ≥3 leptons are: (1) VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} (for validating fake/non-prompt lepton backgrounds), (2) CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z} and CR^Z_{VV} (for estimating t\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson backgrounds), and (3) VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, and VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag} (for validating those estimates).", "section": "Fake/non-prompt lepton background + Estimation of the $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson background in the 3 $\\ell$ selection", "window": 5, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"The text discusses validation regions (VRs) and control regions (CRs) in the context of background estimation. Specifically, Table 4 and Table 6 define regions requiring ≥3 signal leptons. The roles of these regions are described as validating fake/non-prompt lepton backgrounds (VR^Z_{1F}, VR^Z_{2F}), estimating t\\\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson backgrounds (CR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}, CR^Z_{VV}), and validating those estimates (VR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag}).\",\n\"quotes\": [\n\"Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ .\",\n\"Requirement / Region\\nVR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{F}}}$\\n\\nVR ${}^{Z}_{2\\\\textrm{F}}$\",\n\"Number of signal leptons\\n$\\\\geq 3$\",\n\"To validate the background estimates and provide a statistically independent cross-check of the extrapolation to the SRs, three validation regions are defined, as shown in Table 6 . The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\\\\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,}b\\\\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.\",\n\"Requirement / Region\\nCR ${}^{Z}_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}$\\n\\nVR ${}^{Z}_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}$\\n\\nCR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$\\n\\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,n-jet}}$\\n\\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\\\textrm{,}b\\\\textrm{-tag}}$\",\n\"Number of signal leptons\\n$\\\\geq 3$\"\n],\n\"answer\": \"The regions requiring ≥3 leptons are: (1) VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} (for validating fake/non-prompt lepton backgrounds), (2) CR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z} and CR^Z_{VV} (for estimating t\\\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson backgrounds), and (3) VR^Z_{t\\\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, and VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag} (for validating those estimates).\",\n\"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "quotes": [], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The provided text does not mention any regions requiring at least three leptons or their roles.", "section": "Estimation of the $t\\bar{t}$ background in the 1 $\\ell$ selection + Systematic uncertainties", "window": 6, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text discusses regions in the context of the 1 $\\\\ell$ selection (one lepton) and its control/validation regions. No regions requiring at least three leptons are mentioned. The terms 'three leptons' or analogous requirements are absent from the provided text.\", \"quotes\": [], \"answer\": \"The provided text does not mention any regions requiring at least three leptons or their roles.\", \"found\": false}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "quotes": [], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The provided text does not mention any regions requiring at least three leptons.", "section": "Estimation of the $t\\bar{t}$ background in the 1 $\\ell$ selection + Systematic uncertainties", "window": 6, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text discusses regions like the control region CR ${}^{h}_{t\\\\bar{t}}$ and validation regions in the context of the 1 $\\\\ell$ selection (1 lepton). No regions requiring at least three leptons are mentioned in the provided text. The question's criteria (three leptons) are not addressed here.\", \"quotes\": [], \"answer\": \"The provided text does not mention any regions requiring at least three leptons.\", \"found\": false}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": false, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "quotes": [], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The provided text does not mention any regions requiring at least three leptons or their roles.", "section": "Estimation of the $t\\bar{t}$ background in the 1 $\\ell$ selection + Systematic uncertainties", "window": 6, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\"reasoning\": \"The text discusses regions in the 1 $\\\\ell$ selection (e.g., CR ${}^{h}_{t\\\\bar{t}}$ and validation regions) but does not mention any regions requiring at least three leptons. The focus is on jet multiplicity, $b$-tagged jets, and $m_{\\\\textrm{T}}$ requirements, not lepton counts beyond one.\", \"quotes\": [], \"answer\": \"The provided text does not mention any regions requiring at least three leptons or their roles.\", \"found\": false}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 $\\ell$ and 1 $\\ell$ SRs, respectively.", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 $\\ell$ and 1 $\\ell$ SRs, respectively.", "To enhance the exclusion power in the SUSY models considered, a ‘shape-fit’ approach is used where several mutually exclusive bins in different kinematic variables are defined in order to take advantage of the different signal-to-background ratios in the different bins.", "The likelihood is built from the product of probability density functions describing the observed numbers of events in the SR and the associated CRs.", "Table 10 also shows upper limits (at the 95% CL) on the number of BSM events $S^{95}$ , and on the visible BSM cross-section $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{vis}}$", "The statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 ."], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "The 3ℓ SRs (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles include setting exclusion limits for BSM scenarios via upper limits on BSM events and cross-sections (via Tables 10 and 12), and enhancing SUSY model exclusion power through shape-fit analyses of kinematic distributions (as in Figures 6 and 9).", "section": "Results", "window": 7, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n  \"reasoning\": \"The text mentions 'inclusive 3 ℓ SRs' in Tables 10 and the figures, which require at least three leptons. Their roles relate to exclusion limits for BSM scenarios, as described in the context of Tables 10, 12, and the shape-fit approach for SUSY model exclusions.\",\n  \"quotes\": [\n    \"The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 $\\\\ell$ and 1 $\\\\ell$ SRs, respectively.\",\n    \"To enhance the exclusion power in the SUSY models considered, a ‘shape-fit’ approach is used where several mutually exclusive bins in different kinematic variables are defined in order to take advantage of the different signal-to-background ratios in the different bins.\",\n    \"The likelihood is built from the product of probability density functions describing the observed numbers of events in the SR and the associated CRs.\",\n    \"Table 10 also shows upper limits (at the 95% CL) on the number of BSM events $S^{95}$ , and on the visible BSM cross-section $\\\\sigma_{\\\\mathrm{vis}}$\",\n    \"The statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\\\\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 .\"\n  ],\n  \"answer\": \"The 3ℓ SRs (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles include setting exclusion limits for BSM scenarios via upper limits on BSM events and cross-sections (via Tables 10 and 12), and enhancing SUSY model exclusion power through shape-fit analyses of kinematic distributions (as in Figures 6 and 9).\",\n  \"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": null, "quote": "", "quote_verified": false, "quotes": [], "reasoning": "", "answer_text": "", "section": "Results", "window": 7, "escalated": false, "raw": "{\n\"reasoning\": \"The text mentions '3 ℓ' signal regions (SRs) in Tables 10 and the captions of Figures 6 and 8. These regions are part of the analysis requiring at least three leptons. Their roles are described in the context of exclusion limits for BSM scenarios and the shape-fit approach, which uses bins in kinematic variables to enhance exclusion power in SUSY models.\",\n\"quotes\": [\n\"The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 ℓ and 1 ℓ SRs, respectively.\",\n\"Table 10: Observed and expected numbers of events in the 3 ℓ signal regions...\",\n\"Figures 6 and 7 show kinematic distributions after applying all the SR selection requirements except those on E_{\\\\text{T}}^{\\\\text{miss}}, p_{\\\\textrm{T}}^{\\\\ell\\\\ell} or \\\\mathcal{}S and jet multiplicity.\",\n\"SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$\", \"SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}$\", \"SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{A}}}$\", \"SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{B}}}$\" (from Table 10 and related figures)\",\n\"To enhance the exclusion power in the SUSY models considered, a ‘shape-fit’ approach is used where several mutually exclusive bins in different kinematic variables are defined...\",\n\"Figure 10 shows the exclusion limits... obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\\\\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 .\"\n],\n\"answer\": \"The 3 ℓ regions are SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$, SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{B}}}$, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{A}}}$, and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\\\textrm{B}}}$. They require at least three leptons and are used to set exclusion limits for BSM scenarios via the shape-fit approach, which leverages kinematic bins (e.g., $E_{\\\\text{T}}^{\\\\text{miss}}$, $p_{\\\\textrm{T}}^{\\\\ell\\\\ell}$) to distinguish signal from background in SUSY models. Each region's specific kinematic criteria (e.g., $E_{\\\\text{T}}^{\\\\text{miss}}$ ranges in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\\\textrm{A}}}$) contribute to refining exclusion limits by targeting different signal-to-background ratios.\",\n\"found\": true\n}"}, {"paper_id": "2006.05880", "qid": "gf-06", "answer": true, "quote": "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 ℓ and 1 ℓ SRs, respectively.", "quote_verified": true, "quotes": ["The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 ℓ and 1 ℓ SRs, respectively.", "Table 10: Observed and expected numbers of events in the 3 ℓ signal regions...", "Table 12: Selection criteria used in the shape-fit to derive the model-dependent exclusion limits...", "The shape-fit approach is used where several mutually exclusive bins in different kinematic variables are defined in order to take advantage of the different signal-to-background ratios in the different bins.", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}